/34816251?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316","html_url":"https://github.com/dyhan316","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/dyhan316/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2024-12-13T02:39:05Z","updated_at":"2024-12-13T02:46:53Z","body":"I've found that TUEG only has a subset of the channels the channels you mentioned (standard_1020 (not really 1020 haha )) . This implies that either the latent embedding from bipolar channels (ex \"C3-P3\") were 1. provided from other pretraining dataset or 2. the latent embeddings for those channels were trained from scratch (during fine-tuning?) I would love to hear your opinoin/thought on this! \r\n\r\nAlso, could you please explain what you mean by \"absolute spatial embedding method\"?  I thought that LaBraM learns an embedding for each channel separately, and not care about the actual spatial positions of the channels?","author_association":"NONE","pin":null,"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/935963004/LaBraM/issues/comments/2540409453/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":1},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/935963004/LaBraM/issues/comments/2540434999","html_url":"https://github.com/935963004/LaBraM/issues/48#issuecomment-2540434999","issue_url":"https: